\title{genmetalite: Generalized Meta-Analysis of Reduced Regression Models}
\description{
\code{genmetalite} pools regression coefficients across studies that each fit a
\emph{reduced} model containing only some of the covariates in a target \emph{full}
model. Each study's reduced-model coefficient vector is mapped onto the
full-model parameter using covariate second moments, and the mapped estimates
are combined by generalized least squares.
}
\section{Workflow}{

The usual entry point is \code{\link[=calc_linear_beta]{calc_linear_beta()}}, which accepts either
individual-level cohort data or pre-fitted reduced models:

\enumerate{
\item Reduced models are fitted with \code{\link[=run_regression]{run_regression()}} (or supplied by the
user through \code{\link[=as_reduced_model]{as_reduced_model()}}). Each reduced model contributes a
coefficient vector, a robust variance matrix, and the empirical covariance
of its own covariates.
\item \code{\link[=make_sigma_X_ls]{make_sigma_X_ls()}} assembles, for each study, the covariance matrix
of the \emph{full} covariate set. Blocks involving covariates the study did not
measure must come from the reference population; the block among observed
covariates can come either from the study itself
(\code{cov_p_type = "partitioned"}) or from the reference
(\code{cov_p_type = "reference"}).
\item \code{\link[=linear_estimate]{linear_estimate()}} maps and pools, and computes an
influence-function variance.
}

\code{\link[=IVW_estimate]{IVW_estimate()}} and \code{\link[=EB_estimate]{EB_estimate()}} give an inverse-variance weighted
comparator and an empirical-Bayes style blend of the two.
}

\section{Reference reweighting}{

When the reference population does not resemble a study, it can be reweighted
with \code{\link[=ebal_weights]{ebal_weights()}} (entropy balancing) or \code{\link[=ipw_weights]{ipw_weights()}} (inverse
probability of "membership" weighting) before its covariance is computed.
}

\section{Naming conventions}{

Categorical covariates are expanded with \code{\link[stats:model.matrix]{stats::model.matrix()}} throughout,
so a factor \code{edu} with levels \verb{1, 2, 3} yields columns \code{edu2} and \code{edu3}.
Coefficient names, covariance dimnames and reference-data column names must
agree; the package matches by name rather than position everywhere, and
errors rather than silently misaligning.
}

